  Reading: CN=0 / MCI=1 / AD=2. Disease-axis coordinate axis(p) = p(MCI)·1 + p(AD)·2;
        Δaxis = axis(after occlusion) − axis(before occlusion): negative = movement towards the healthy end, positive = movement towards the disease end.

[disease-axis coordinate check] baseline axis means: CN 0.1766 / MCI 0.9575 / AD 1.9052 (should be strictly increasing)

  Δaxis by condition (nonsat, negative = towards the healthy end):
    MTL_d100_F1const         all -0.13403 | CN -0.08302  MCI -0.08642  AD -0.23606
    MTL_d100_F2noise         all -0.05848 | CN +0.00912  MCI -0.02970  AD -0.15409
    MTL_d100_F3transplant    all -0.05463 | CN +0.02855  MCI +0.00482  AD -0.20000

[joint test] statistic = mean(sign(donor−recipient) × Δaxis)   (the closer the donor to the disease end → the more the recipient moves towards the disease end)
  F1 constant fill (donor = healthy side, s≡−1): stat = +0.13403, Wilcoxon p = 4.557e-08, n=80
  F3 cross-subject transplant (donor direction random): stat = +0.12416, Wilcoxon p = 1.4e-06, n=80
  permutation null distribution (n=2000, donor labels resampled at random): mean +0.07342  SD 0.02088  95th percentile +0.10806
  one-sided permutation p = 0.0045
  → ✅ supports "MTL carries disease-axis information and its direction can be determined by donor pathology"

